<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<handout xml:id="worksheet-8">
  <title>Worksheet 8: Sections 4.7, 1.3, 3.4, and 3.5 - Newton's Method, Derivatives of Trig Functions, and Derivatives as Rates of Change</title>

  <page>
    <paragraphs xml:id="ws8-prob1">
      <title>Problem 1</title>
      <p>
        Remember the <em>Intermediate Value Theorem (IVT)?</em> It says: If <m>f</m> is a continuous function on the closed interval <m>[a,b]</m>, then <m>f</m> attains every value between <m>f(a)</m> and <m>f(b)</m> somewhere on the interval <m>[a,b]</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        This theorem and Newton's method can work together to tell us about roots of really unruly functions like
        <me>f(x) = x^{5} - \frac{5}{2}x^2 + x - \frac{3}{2}.</me>
      </p>

      <sidebyside widths="38% 58%" valign="top">
        <image source="pictures/newtongeometry.jpg">
          <shortdescription>
            Graph illustrating Newton's method on a polynomial curve with a tangent line approximation from x_0 to x_1 near the root.
          </shortdescription>
        </image>
        <stack>
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                How do we know we aren't wasting our time looking for a root? The IVT can tell us that one exists. Fill in the blanks:
              </p>
              <p>
                Since <m>f(x)</m> is <fillin characters="16"/> on <m>[0,2]</m> and
                <me>f(0) = \underline{\hspace{0.5in}} \lt \underline{\hspace{0.5in}} \lt f(2) = \underline{\hspace{0.5in}},</me>
                the IVT guarantees that there is at least one solution to the equation <m>f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="6"/>. That is, <m>f(x)</m> has a <fillin characters="10"/> on <m>[0,2]</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li workspace="1in">
              <p>
                Now that we know there is a root, we can use Newton's method to estimate it. Use <m>x_0 = 1</m> to find an estimate <m>x_1</m> of the root.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li workspace="1in">
              <p>
                Examine the graph of <m>f(x)</m>. Is <m>x_1</m> closer to the root in <m>[0,2]</m>? Can you find a different choice of <m>x_0</m> that might work better to approximate the root of <m>f</m>? Find <m>x_1</m> using the graph of <m>f(x)</m> and Newton's method with your chosen <m>x_0</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </stack>
      </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="ws8-prob2">
      <title>Problem 2</title>
      <p>
        Do the following:
      </p>
      <ol marker="i)">
        <li workspace="1.1in">
          <p>
            Find the derivative of <m>\displaystyle f(t) = \frac{\cos(\sin(t))}{\sec(t)}</m>. You do not need to simplify your answer.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li workspace="0.8in">
          <p>
            Consider <m>g(x) = x \tan(x)</m>. Which one of the following options represents <m>g'(x)</m>?
          </p>
          <sidebyside widths="24% 22% 24% 24%" valign="middle">
            <p>(A) <m>\dfrac{\sin(x)\cos(x) + x}{\cos^2(x)}</m></p>
            <p>(B) <m>\dfrac{1}{\cos^2(x)}</m></p>
            <p>(C) <m>\dfrac{\sin(x) + x}{\cos^2(x)}</m></p>
            <p>(D) <m>\dfrac{\sin(x)\cos(x) + 1}{\cos^2(x)}</m></p>
          </sidebyside>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="ws8-prob3">
      <title>Problem 3</title>
      <p>
        A culture of yeast cells is introduced to a growth medium. For the first two hours (<m>0 \leq t \leq 2</m>), the population of the culture at time <m>t</m> (in hours) is modeled by
        <me>P(t) = 10{,}000 + 500t + \frac{1000}{\pi}\cos(\pi t).</me>
      </p>
      <ol marker="a)">
        <li workspace="0.8in">
          <p>Find the rate of change of the population, <m>P'(t)</m>, at any time <m>t</m>.</p>
        </li>
        <li workspace="1in">
          <p>At what exact time <m>t</m> during the first two hours (<m>0 \leq t \leq 2</m>) does the population stop increasing for the first time?</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </paragraphs>
  </page>

  <page>
    <paragraphs xml:id="ws8-extra-heading">
      <title>Additional Practice Problems (to be completed outside of studio)</title>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="ws8-extra-prob4">
      <title>Problem 4</title>
      <p>
        Consider the equation <m>x^3 = x^2 + 2</m>.
      </p>
      <ol marker="a)">
        <li workspace="0.7in">
          <p>Write down an equation <m>f(x)</m> that you can use Newton's method on in order to estimate a solution of the equation above.</p>
        </li>
        <li workspace="1.1in">
          <p>Using the initial guess <m>x_0 = 1</m>, find at least two further estimates (<m>x_1</m> and <m>x_2</m>) of a solution to this equation. You may use a calculator if it's helpful.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="ws8-extra-prob5">
      <title>Problem 5</title>
      <p workspace="1.1in">
        Suppose we want to use Newton's method to approximate a solution to the equation <m>x^2 = 7</m>. Find and simplify the recursive formula for <m>x_{n+1}</m>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="ws8-extra-prob6">
      <title>Problem 6</title>
      <p>
        A rock thrown vertically upward from the surface of the moon at a velocity of <m>24</m> m/sec reaches a height of <m>s = 24t - 0.8t^2</m> m in <m>t</m> sec.
      </p>
      <ol marker="a)">
        <li workspace="0.8in">
          <p>Find the rock's velocity and acceleration at time <m>t</m>. (The acceleration in this case is the acceleration of gravity on the moon.)</p>
        </li>
        <li workspace="0.7in">
          <p>How long does it take the rock to reach its highest point?</p>
        </li>
        <li workspace="0.7in">
          <p>How high does the rock go?</p>
        </li>
        <li workspace="0.7in">
          <p>How long is the rock aloft?</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="ws8-extra-prob7">
      <title>Problem 7</title>
      <p>
        Find the derivative of each of the following functions. You may need to use multiple rules, and it will help you to have a plan. Be sure to practice careful notation and organized work! You do not need to simplify your answers.
      </p>
      <ol marker="(a)">
        <li workspace="0.8in">
          <p><m>f(x) = \sin(x)\tan(x)</m></p>
        </li>
        <li workspace="0.9in">
          <p><m>\displaystyle g(x) = \frac{\sec(x)}{\csc(x)}</m></p>
        </li>
        <li workspace="1in">
          <p><m>g(t) = \csc(t)\cot(t)\cos(\tan(t))</m></p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="ws8-extra-prob8">
      <title>Problem 8</title>
      <p>
        The first quadrant of the unit circle is shown below. Fill in the blanks and make sure you understand the angles and the corresponding values of the trigonometric functions for each point. Other common angles, which we mentioned when reviewing sine and cosine functions, are <m>\pi</m> and <m>2\pi</m>.
      </p>

      <sidebyside width="55%">
        <image source="pictures/Quadrant 1.png">
          <shortdescription>
            First quadrant of the unit circle with blank boxes for standard angles in degrees and radians and their corresponding (cos, sin) coordinates.
          </shortdescription>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>
  </page>
</handout>